import"./NZTpNUN0.js";import"./BkvxQF8z.js";import{t as i,P as o,q as n}from"./D3cAVrQp.js";var a=i("<h1>Planetary Health Governance Framework v0.9.1</h1> <h2>Cross-Domain Synthesis, Participatory Audit, Public-Health Coherence, and Strategic Recommendation</h2> <p><strong>Controlled successor to:</strong> Planetary Health Governance Framework v0.8<br> <strong>Framework identifier:</strong> <code>framework_planetary_health</code><br> <strong>Interface conformance:</strong> <code>CERGTA/0.1</code><br> <strong>Role-separation counterpart:</strong> Ecological Intelligence, Observation, and Interpretation Layer v2.7.1<br> <strong>Status:</strong> Constitutional role-separation successor — controlled draft — cluster-conformant patch <strong>Date:</strong> 2026-08-01</p> <hr> <h2>0. Executive summary</h2> <p>Planetary health is a coordination problem across human health, animal health, ecosystems, culture, infrastructure, and future generations. This framework provides a strategic forum for seeing those interactions, auditing cross-domain effects, enabling affected communities to challenge system-level conclusions, and issuing transparent recommendations to competent institutions.</p> <p>It does not own ecological observation, recognize ecosystem rights, appoint guardians, bind other frameworks, condition essential funding, impose sanctions, declare emergencies, command specialized responders, adjudicate disputes, or enforce compliance.</p> <blockquote><p><strong>Synthesis is not supremacy. Audit is not investigation. Recommendation is not command. A planetary view must not erase local authority, distributional harm, or uncertainty.</strong></p></blockquote> <h3>Core institutional split</h3> <ul><li>The <strong>Ecological Intelligence Commons</strong> maintains ecological methods and evidence.</li> <li>The <strong>Planetary Health Synthesis Office (PHSO)</strong> independently compiles the Biosphere Health Index and its modules.</li> <li>The <strong>Planetary Health Council (PHC)</strong> interprets published evidence, conducts advisory audits, and issues strategic recommendations.</li> <li><strong>Planetary Health Assemblies</strong> and the <strong>Future Generations Forum</strong> challenge assumptions, distributions, and long-horizon effects.</li> <li>Competent legal, sectoral, financial, emergency, and operational authorities decide whether and how to act.</li></ul> <hr> <h1>1. Planetary Health Charter</h1> <p>Planetary health governance is grounded in:</p> <ol><li>the interdependence of human, animal, ecosystem, and community health;</li> <li>health and essential ecological conditions as public goods and commons;</li> <li>prevention and precaution without automatic coercion;</li> <li>epistemic justice and affected-nation authority;</li> <li>intergenerational and interspecies responsibility;</li> <li>distributional justice—averages cannot hide concentrated harm;</li> <li>plurality of cultural and spiritual meaning without binding metaphysical doctrine;</li> <li>subsidiarity and lawful role separation;</li> <li>reversible, reviewable, and correctable governance;</li> <li>protection of future agency rather than prescription of one future.</li></ol> <hr> <h1>2. Source freeze and legal character</h1> <table><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>SHA-256</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ecological Intelligence & Rights Layer v2.6</td><td>6f72cf03524f2d84240d509eb9ed5c9d9f95914c21769c2ed2dc5440d36dee12</td></tr><tr><td>Planetary Health Governance Framework v0.8</td><td>048d602f56cbbddb76ed24693b22535f4aeaadb849789cbd5e3cf2ed27432999</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA/0.1</td><td>3c45e37c834c727471657fc91149edbe3011137562c2dd73e54b20226c92589b</td></tr><tr><td>Commons/Ecological Authority Audit v0.1</td><td>974dfd8dd4bf5b623bd5e7e544b7afdbbd89869b66f107db293cfe6af67a043b</td></tr></tbody></table> <p>This table is a <strong>provenance record</strong>: it identifies the exact texts read and audited when this successor was drafted. It is not the operative dependency set. The instruments this patch interoperates with are listed in Appendix A.1, and the authoritative machine-readable set is the cluster registry named in A.4. Where the two differ, A.1 governs. Digests are content hashes and remain valid under renaming; a filename here identifies an instrument, not a storage location.</p> <p>PHG is a strategic, coordinative, participatory, and advisory framework. Its findings and recommendations have no self-executing legal, financial, territorial, custodial, coercive, emergency, or operational effect.</p> <p>A recipient must independently establish its legal source, jurisdiction, adoption status, action class, safeguards, review, and remedy. Silence is not acceptance.</p> <hr> <h1>3. Role separation</h1> <h2>3.1 Ecological Intelligence</h2> <p>EIOI owns ecological observation methods, ecological indicators, model registries, causal assessment, alerts, and ecological module handoffs. PHG cannot alter source evidence or direct a predetermined result.</p> <h2>3.2 Planetary Health Synthesis Office</h2> <p>PHSO is technically and administratively independent from PHC deliberation. It compiles modules under a published methodology and cannot recommend policy. Its governing charter protects professional independence, conflict disclosure, correction, open methods, low-tech participation, and protected data.</p> <h2>3.3 Planetary Health Council</h2> <p>PHC receives published evidence, conducts system-level audits, identifies interactions and blind spots, convenes dialogue, and issues recommendations. PHC cannot issue binding directives, sanctions, funding freezes, emergency orders, guardian appointments, rights decisions, or operational commands.</p> <h2>3.4 Assemblies and future generations</h2> <p>Planetary Health Assemblies and the Future Generations Forum have rights to information, explanation, challenge, reconsideration, dissent, and independent review. They do not possess automatic vetoes over persons, property, budgets, permits, or projects merely because a threshold is crossed.</p> <h2>3.5 Specialized frameworks</h2> <p>Public health, animal welfare, biodiversity, water, oceans, soil, technology, finance, justice, emergency, and other specialized frameworks retain their lawful roles. PHG coordinates; it does not absorb them.</p> <hr> <h1>4. Governance architecture</h1> <h2>4.1 Planetary Health Council</h2> <p>PHC composition should include Earth-systems science, public health, animal health, Indigenous and affected-community representatives, ethics, youth, disability expertise, labour, and regional diversity. Members serve fixed terms, disclose conflicts, and may be recalled or removed under a published process.</p> <p>PHC deliberations must identify which statements are scientific findings, normative judgments, scenario assumptions, strategic recommendations, or unresolved questions.</p> <h2>4.2 Planetary Health Synthesis Office</h2> <p>PHSO maintains:</p> <ul><li>the module registry;</li> <li>the BHI methodology charter;</li> <li>versioned releases;</li> <li>uncertainty and dissent records;</li> <li>correction propagation;</li> <li>accessible public explanations;</li> <li>protected-data controls;</li> <li>historical reproducibility.</li></ul> <h2>4.3 Planetary Health Assemblies</h2> <p>Assemblies are affected-community review bodies. They may require PHC and PHSO to explain methods, correct local representation, include missing evidence, publish dissent, or commission independent review. An assembly cannot speak for an affected Indigenous nation unless that nation expressly authorizes it.</p> <h2>4.4 Future Generations Forum</h2> <p>The Forum conducts long-horizon impact review. It may place a recommendation under reconsideration and require a published response. It may not claim to represent future persons as a sovereign constituency or create a general veto.</p> <h2>4.5 Independent Audit and Methodology Review Board</h2> <p>This board reviews conflicts, audit fairness, BHI methods, model governance, and complaints. It cannot decide the substantive policy response or exercise enforcement powers.</p> <hr> <h1>5. Biosphere Health Index constitution</h1> <h2>5.1 Purpose</h2> <p>The BHI is a modular synthesis and navigation tool. It helps decision-makers see interactions among ecological integrity, human health, animal health, social conditions, and distributional burdens.</p> <h2>5.2 Non-purpose</h2> <p>The BHI is not:</p> <ul><li>a replacement for GDP by legal fiat;</li> <li>a legal threshold for rights, vetoes, permits, sanctions, or funding;</li> <li>proof of causation, compliance, guilt, stewardship success, or community worth;</li> <li>a licence to compare culturally incommensurable values without explanation;</li> <li>a single optimization objective for civilization.</li></ul> <h2>5.3 Module architecture</h2> <p>Each module retains its own custodian, method, version, units, uncertainty, and access rules. A composite release must show the modules and distributions. Non-derogable essential minimums and catastrophic localized harm cannot be averaged away.</p> <h2>5.4 Weighting</h2> <p>Weighting is an explicit normative choice. PHSO conducts a public process, publishes sensitivity analysis, records dissent, and keeps alternative views available. PHC cannot secretly or retroactively change weights.</p> <h2>5.5 Cultural and spiritual measures</h2> <p>LMCI, cultural vitality, sacred relationship, and spiritual well-being may be included only as voluntary, context-specific, non-diagnostic evidence. They cannot be used to rank communities, infer developmental stage, justify intervention, or condition rights and resources.</p> <hr> <h1>6. Planetary Health Audits</h1> <p>An audit identifies cross-framework effects and recommends correction. It is not an investigation, adjudication, or compliance order.</p> <p>Every audit SHALL include:</p> <ul><li>a published question and scope;</li> <li>source and legal character;</li> <li>conflict disclosures;</li> <li>evidence and access rules;</li> <li>affected-framework and affected-community notice;</li> <li>answer, correction, context, and dissent rights;</li> <li>distributional and affected-nation analysis;</li> <li>uncertainty and alternative explanations;</li> <li>draft review;</li> <li>final finding and recommendation separation;</li> <li>expiry, follow-up, correction, and closure.</li></ul> <p>PHC may request information. Compulsory inspection, confidential seizure, witness compulsion, or evidentiary investigation requires separate authority.</p> <hr> <h1>7. Recommendations and adoption</h1> <p>PHC recommendations use explicit states:</p> <ul><li><code>draft</code>;</li> <li><code>published_advisory</code>;</li> <li><code>offered_to_recipient</code>;</li> <li><code>received_not_assessed</code>;</li> <li><code>accepted_for_review</code>;</li> <li><code>partially_accepted</code>;</li> <li><code>rejected_with_reasons</code>;</li> <li><code>implemented_under_external_authority</code>;</li> <li><code>superseded</code>;</li> <li><code>closed</code>.</li></ul> <p>A recommendation becomes binding only through the recipient’s lawful CERGTA, treaty, domestic, contractual, or institutional adoption route.</p> <hr> <h1>8. Rights, guardianship, and commons boundaries</h1> <p>PHC may recommend that a competent body open ecological-rights review. It cannot recognize a rights-holder, define a legal boundary, appoint or instruct guardians, designate sacredness, transfer title, establish a trust, revoke stewardship, or determine compensation.</p> <p>The International Tribunal for the Rights of Nature, if lawfully constituted elsewhere, is not a PHG institution and receives no jurisdiction from a PHC referral alone.</p> <hr> <h1>9. Future generations and procedural challenge</h1> <p>The Future Generations Forum identifies irreversible risk, option foreclosure, closure debt, long time horizons, and distribution across generations. Its output is a reasoned challenge requiring response and reconsideration.</p> <p>No numerical threshold creates an automatic youth veto. A lawful jurisdiction may separately enact veto or consent rights, but must identify scope, authority, standing, review, override, expiry, and remedy.</p> <hr> <h1>10. Indigenous health sovereignty and epistemic justice</h1> <p>Affected Indigenous nations retain authority over their health systems, Traditional Knowledge, sacred relations, territories, participation, consent, representation, and data.</p> <p>Earth Council or global Indigenous representation may advise and audit process integrity. It cannot consent, waive rights, disclose knowledge, designate sacredness, or speak for another nation.</p> <hr> <h1>11. Emergency boundary</h1> <p>The former PHC “binding intervention authority,” 90-day emergency power, supermajority operational override, and automatic real-time data sharing are retired.</p> <p>PHC may issue <code>PHG-24</code> and refer a possible emergency to CDEE/ECRC. Emergency authorities independently decide declaration, command, resource mobilization, action, renewal, termination, and remedy.</p> <p>PHSO and PHC may share minimum-necessary information under <code>PHG-25</code>. Health, ecological, asylum, Indigenous, survivor, and protected data do not automatically migrate into crisis or security systems.</p> <hr> <h1>12. Funding and incentive firewall</h1> <p>PHC may identify funding needs, distributional injustice, and priority gaps. It may not allocate GCF resources, impose levies, freeze funding, direct Hearts or Leaves, or condition essential services on BHI performance.</p> <p>Funding authorities must distinguish stewardship effort from ecological outcome, and local action from upstream, historical, climatic, or transboundary drivers. Communities must not be punished for inherited or externally caused harm.</p> <hr> <h1>13. Technology and precaution</h1> <p>PHC may identify health and biospheric concerns related to AI, biotechnology, synthetic biology, nanotechnology, or other technologies. Regulatory decisions, moratoria, deployment conditions, shutdown, containment, and enforcement belong to TGIF, PIS, justice, or other competent authorities.</p> <p>No PHC subcommittee receives a general kill switch or containment mandate.</p> <hr> <h1>14. Health commons and essential minimums</h1> <p>PHG treats clean air, water, sanitation, soil, seeds, food, habitat, public health, and necessary care as shared foundations of flourishing. It may recommend protection but cannot itself nationalize, transfer, decommodify, allocate, ration, or restrict a resource.</p> <p>Every downstream action must preserve essential minimums and non-deprivation under CERGTA-36 and CERGTA-38.</p> <hr> <h1>15. Education, dialogue, and public communication</h1> <p>Planetary Health Literacy, cultural dialogue, spiritual dialogue, citizen science, and professional training are voluntary, plural, accessible, and non-doctrinal. Public materials must distinguish aspiration, evidence, recommendation, adopted law, and operational authority.</p> <p>A public ratification tracker, target, campaign, or endorsement count cannot activate legal power or preferential essential funding.</p> <hr> <h1>16. Cross-framework interfaces</h1> <ul><li><strong>EIOI:</strong> source of ecological modules and alerts.</li> <li><strong>Global Health and pandemic frameworks:</strong> source of human-health evidence and competent operational response.</li> <li><strong>Animal welfare:</strong> source of animal-health and welfare evidence.</li> <li><strong>Biodiversity, Water, Oceans, Soil, and Hearthstone:</strong> competent implementation frameworks receiving advisory findings.</li> <li><strong>CERGTA:</strong> controls rights, guardianship, title, stewardship, allocation, restriction, finance, adjudication, and restoration routes.</li> <li><strong>CDEE/ECRC:</strong> controls emergency identity and coordination.</li> <li><strong>SCPA:</strong> controls coercive execution and security-facing action.</li> <li><strong>TGIF and Oracle:</strong> control technology and digital-sentience questions; PHG supplies biospheric-health analysis only.</li> <li><strong>MOS:</strong> supplies advisory ethical reflection, never legal status or scientific validation.</li></ul> <hr> <h1>17. Common record inheritance</h1> <p>Every PHG record inherits the forty CERGTA header fields:</p> <p><code>record_id</code>, <code>record_instance_id</code>, <code>record_version</code>, <code>status</code>, <code>created_at</code>, <code>effective_at</code>, <code>created_by</code>, <code>competent_authority</code>, <code>legal_source</code>, <code>jurisdiction</code>, <code>authority_activation_state</code>, <code>ecological_action_cluster_id</code>, <code>action_class</code>, <code>exact_act</code>, <code>entity_asset_resource_id</code>, <code>ecological_and_legal_boundary_version</code>, <code>affected_persons_and_communities</code>, <code>affected_indigenous_nations</code>, <code>claimants_and_interest_holders</code>, <code>linked_records</code>, <code>source_and_reliability_limits</code>, <code>uncertainty_and_dissent</code>, <code>prohibited_inferences</code>, <code>rights_and_safeguards</code>, <code>essential_minimums</code>, <code>consent_fpic_and_nonengagement_status</code>, <code>access_class</code>, <code>notice_status</code>, <code>review_authority</code>, <code>review_due_at</code>, <code>expires_at_or_nonexpiry_basis</code>, <code>challenge_and_interim_relief_route</code>, <code>termination_trigger</code>, <code>authority_asset_and_credential_return</code>, <code>data_disposition_rule</code>, <code>remedy_route</code>, <code>supersedes</code>, <code>superseded_by</code>, <code>integrity_digest</code>, <code>public_summary_or_protected_basis</code>.</p> <p>The record must clearly state when the competent authority is PHC, PHSO, an Assembly, an independent reviewer, or an external recipient.</p> <hr> <h1>18. Canonical PHG records</h1> <p><strong>Required boundary for every record below.</strong> Each record inherits the forty-field CERGTA header. It SHALL identify the competent technical or strategic function, purpose, source and reliability limits, uncertainty and dissent, access class, review and expiry, challenge, correction, downstream recipients, data disposition, and prohibited inferences. It creates no authority outside the exact role described.</p> <table><thead><tr><th>ID</th><th>Record</th><th>Function</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>PHG-01</code></td><td>Constituting Instrument, Role Boundary, and Non-Authority Register</td><td>Establishes PHC, the independent synthesis office, assemblies, source versions, and powers expressly withheld.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PHG-02</code></td><td>Planetary Health Module Registry</td><td>Lists ecological, human-health, animal-health, social, cultural, and distributional modules and their custodians.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PHG-03</code></td><td>BHI Methodology Charter</td><td>Defines purpose, non-purpose, module architecture, version, weighting process, non-compensation rules, uncertainty, and prohibited legal uses.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PHG-04</code></td><td>Module Intake and Acceptance</td><td>Records source, version, quality, uncertainty, access limits, acceptance, deficiency, or rejection by the synthesis office.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PHG-05</code></td><td>Weighting, Value Judgment, and Dissent</td><td>Makes normative weighting choices visible and records minority reports, affected-community objections, and non-aggregation views.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PHG-06</code></td><td>BHI Release and Public Explanation</td><td>Publishes the composite, modules, distributions, uncertainty, revision status, and a plain-language non-authority warning.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PHG-07</code></td><td>Distributional and Vulnerability Analysis</td><td>Prevents averages from masking burden concentration across communities, species, territories, generations, and social groups.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PHG-08</code></td><td>Synthesis Uncertainty and Conflict Record</td><td>Records uncertainty, disagreement among modules, causal limits, missing evidence, and unresolved trade-offs.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PHG-09</code></td><td>Planetary Health Audit Initiation</td><td>Defines audit scope, legal character, questions, affected frameworks, evidence rights, conflicts, and schedule.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PHG-10</code></td><td>Audit Evidence Request and Response</td><td>Requests evidence without search, seizure, compulsory disclosure, or investigative authority unless separately authorized.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PHG-11</code></td><td>Affected Framework and Community Response</td><td>Preserves answer rights, correction, context, distributional evidence, Indigenous authority, and dissent.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PHG-12</code></td><td>Planetary Health Audit Finding</td><td>States supported findings, uncertainty, materiality, alternatives, and prohibited inferences without binding consequence.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PHG-13</code></td><td>Strategic Recommendation</td><td>Recommends action to competent frameworks and identifies the CERGTA or other legal route required before consequence.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PHG-14</code></td><td>Implementation Response and Follow-Up</td><td>Records whether the competent recipient accepts, partially accepts, rejects, or proposes alternatives and what PHC duties remain.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PHG-15</code></td><td>Ecological Rights-Review Referral</td><td>Forwards a bounded CERGTA-11/12 package; PHC cannot recognize rights or appoint guardians.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PHG-16</code></td><td>Standard, Plan, or Policy Adoption Referral</td><td>Routes recommendations to CERGTA-35 or the recipient’s lawful adoption process.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PHG-17</code></td><td>Funding and Incentive Recommendation Firewall</td><td>Separates public-interest analysis from actual grants, conditions, bonuses, freezes, levies, or sanctions.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PHG-18</code></td><td>Future Generations Impact Review</td><td>Provides procedural challenge, long-horizon analysis, and reconsideration; it creates no automatic veto.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PHG-19</code></td><td>Planetary Health Assembly Challenge</td><td>Allows affected assemblies to require explanation, correction, reconsideration, or independent review without acquiring general jurisdiction.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PHG-20</code></td><td>Affected Indigenous Nation and Health Sovereignty Interface</td><td>Recognizes that affected nations govern their own health systems, knowledge, consent, territory, and representation.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PHG-21</code></td><td>Health Commons Risk and Essential-Minimum Referral</td><td>Identifies risks to water, air, soil, seeds, sanitation, food, habitat, and health access while preserving CERGTA essential-minimum and service-continuity routes.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PHG-22</code></td><td>Cross-Domain Risk Synthesis</td><td>Integrates ecological, public-health, animal-health, social, and economic evidence without treating correlation or a composite score as causation.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PHG-23</code></td><td>Public Health–Ecological Coordination Request</td><td>Requests coordination from specialized frameworks while leaving operational authority with those frameworks.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PHG-24</code></td><td>Emergency Alert and CDEE/ECRC Referral</td><td>Communicates a possible emergency to the emergency stack; it does not declare an emergency or activate response powers.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PHG-25</code></td><td>Protected Crisis Information Handoff</td><td>Controls minimum-necessary, purpose-limited information sharing during emergencies; no automatic real-time data sharing.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PHG-26</code></td><td>Technology Ethics and Precaution Referral</td><td>Provides ethical and health analysis to TGIF or another competent technology authority without creating a ban, kill switch, or deployment authorization.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PHG-27</code></td><td>Social Cohesion, Mental Health, and Cultural Well-Being Assessment</td><td>Produces contextual evidence and recommendations; no psychological, spiritual, or cultural score may create adverse legal treatment.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PHG-28</code></td><td>Literacy, Dialogue, and Capacity Programme</td><td>Coordinates voluntary education, public communication, and training without doctrinal compulsion.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PHG-29</code></td><td>Correction, Revision, and Downstream Propagation</td><td>Corrects BHI releases, audits, recommendations, and derivative uses and notifies all recipients.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PHG-30</code></td><td>Complaint, Independent Review, and Remedy Interface</td><td>Provides complaints and correction within PHG and routes legal relief to the competent body.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PHG-31</code></td><td>Method, Mandate, and Programme Termination</td><td>Ends methods, audits, councils, or programmes and returns authority, data, credentials, funds, and unresolved duties.</td></tr><tr><td><code>PHG-32</code></td><td>Unresolved Planetary Health Duty Custody</td><td>Keeps unresolved harm, uncertainty, or follow-up visible without preserving emergency, funding, or coercive authority.</td></tr></tbody></table> <h1>19. CERGTA crosswalk</h1> <table><thead><tr><th>CERGTA record</th><th>Canonical function</th><th>Relationship</th><th>Domain record</th><th>Role boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CERGTA-01</td><td>Constituting Instrument, Legal Source, Jurisdiction, and Activation Status</td><td>implements</td><td>PHG-01</td><td>Constituting and non-authority register.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-02</td><td>Ecological Entity Candidate, Resource, Asset, and Boundary Intake</td><td>splits</td><td>PHG-02/PHG-04</td><td>Receives entity and resource identifiers from competent custodians; does not constitute legal boundaries.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-03</td><td>Observation, Monitoring, Sampling, and Provenance Record</td><td>imports</td><td>EIOI-04 and competent health modules</td><td>PHG does not originate ecological observation; it receives versioned modules.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-04</td><td>Ecological Data Purpose, Consent, Sovereignty, Access, and Retention Record</td><td>extends</td><td>PHG-04/PHG-25</td><td>Purpose, access, sovereignty, and onward-use controls for received modules.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-05</td><td>Indicator, Index, Threshold, Weighting, and Model Specification</td><td>implements</td><td>PHG-03/PHG-05/PHG-06</td><td>BHI methodology, weighting, release, and non-authority warning.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-06</td><td>Ecological Interpretation, Causal Assessment, and Uncertainty Record</td><td>implements</td><td>PHG-07/PHG-08/PHG-22</td><td>Cross-domain interpretation and uncertainty; no legal attribution.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-07</td><td>Traditional Knowledge, Sacred Knowledge, and Epistemic Contribution Record</td><td>extends</td><td>PHG-20/PHG-27</td><td>Protected epistemic contributions and cultural limits.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-08</td><td>Affected Persons, Communities, Nations, Users, Workers, and Rights Map</td><td>implements</td><td>PHG-07/PHG-11</td><td>Affected-community and distributional map for audits.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-09</td><td>Affected Indigenous Nation and Applicable-Law Determination</td><td>imports</td><td>CERGTA-09 via PHG-20</td><td>Affected-nation authority is external and controlling.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-10</td><td>Notice, Participation, Accessibility, Counsel, and Support Record</td><td>implements</td><td>PHG-11/PHG-19/PHG-30</td><td>Notice, participation, accessibility, challenge, and review.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-11</td><td>Ecological Rights Review Petition and Standing Record</td><td>extends</td><td>PHG-15</td><td>May refer a rights-review petition; no standing determination.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-12</td><td>Rights Review Recommendation and Dissent Record</td><td>extends</td><td>PHG-15</td><td>Advisory recommendation and dissent.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-13</td><td>Lawful Ecological Rights Recognition Decision</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-13</td><td>No rights-recognition authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-14</td><td>Rights Catalogue, Priority, Conflict, and Non-Derogation Record</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-14</td><td>No rights catalogue or priority authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-15</td><td>Ecological and Legal Boundary Constitution Record</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-15</td><td>No legal boundary constitution.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-16</td><td>Guardian Nomination and Eligibility Record</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-16</td><td>No guardian nomination authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-17</td><td>Guardian Appointment, Mandate, and Standing Record</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-17</td><td>No guardian appointment or standing.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-18</td><td>Guardian Fiduciary Duty, Instructions, Conflict, and Recusal Record</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-18</td><td>No guardian instruction authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-19</td><td>Guardian Review, Complaint, Removal, Substitution, and Liability Record</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-19</td><td>No guardian removal or liability authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-20</td><td>Sacred, Cultural, Biocultural, and Inalienability Designation Petition</td><td>splits</td><td>PHG-20/PHG-27</td><td>May support a petition or cultural-health evidence; designation is external.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-21</td><td>Sacred, Cultural, Biocultural, and Inalienability Designation Decision</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-21</td><td>No sacred, cultural, or inalienability designation.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-22</td><td>Claim Intake, Standing, and Relief Requested Record</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-22</td><td>No claims adjudication.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-23</td><td>Competing Claims, Parties, Interests, and Non-Extinguishment Map</td><td>splits</td><td>PHG-07/PHG-11</td><td>Maps affected interests for audit; legal claims map is external.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-24</td><td>Historical, Customary, Occupancy, Use, and Documentary Evidence Record</td><td>extends</td><td>PHG-10/PHG-11</td><td>May receive historical and contextual evidence without assigning legal weight.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-25</td><td>Interim Non-Prejudice and Anti-Dissipation Protection Record</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-25</td><td>No interim asset restraint.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-26</td><td>Title, Tenure, Trust, Covenant, Licence, and Encumbrance Baseline Record</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-26</td><td>No title or tenure authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-27</td><td>Transfer, Rematriation, Trust Constitution, and Conveyance Authorization</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-27</td><td>No rematriation or transfer authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-28</td><td>Compensation, Reparations, Debt, Tenant, Worker, User, and Service Continuity Record</td><td>splits</td><td>PHG-07/PHG-17/PHG-21</td><td>May identify burdens and continuity needs; compensation decisions are external.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-29</td><td>Commons, Title, Rights, Guardian, Permit, and Stewardship Registry Entry</td><td>splits</td><td>PHG-02/PHG-06</td><td>Publishes analytical identifiers only; no legal registry effect.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-30</td><td>Registry Error, Identity Collision, Boundary Conflict, and Correction Record</td><td>extends</td><td>PHG-29</td><td>Must propagate corrections to every legal registry or recipient.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-31</td><td>Stewardship Charter, Purpose, Powers, Duties, and Prohibited Acts</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-31</td><td>No stewardship charter authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-32</td><td>Stewardship Plan, Ecological Objective, Budget, and Participation Record</td><td>splits</td><td>PHG-13/PHG-16</td><td>Strategic recommendation only; plan and budget adoption external.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-33</td><td>Stewardship Performance, Audit, Metric-Gaming, and Context Record</td><td>splits</td><td>PHG-09–PHG-14</td><td>Planetary health audit is advisory and does not equal stewardship performance adjudication.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-34</td><td>Stewardship Correction, Suspension, Revocation, Succession, and Dissolution Record</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-34</td><td>No stewardship suspension or revocation.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-35</td><td>Ecological Standard, Target, Plan, and Legal Adoption Status Record</td><td>extends</td><td>PHG-16</td><td>Recommendation routed to lawful adoption; no automatic status change.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-36</td><td>Resource Priority, Essential Minimum, Allocation Method, and Scarcity Record</td><td>splits</td><td>PHG-21</td><td>Essential-minimum risk analysis; allocation authority external.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-37</td><td>Quota, Permit, Licence, Tariff, Bond, and Use-Condition Decision</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-37</td><td>No quota, permit, licence, tariff, or bond authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-38</td><td>Commercial Restriction, Service Continuity, and Non-Deprivation Record</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-38</td><td>No commercial restriction or service cut-off authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-39</td><td>Inspection Authorization, Entry, Sampling, and Report Record</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-39</td><td>Audit evidence requests are not compulsory inspections.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-40</td><td>Ecological Investigation Opening, Methods, Parties, and Closure Record</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-40</td><td>PHG audits are not legal investigations.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-41</td><td>Ecological Evidence Custody, Scientific Attribution, and Admissibility Record</td><td>splits</td><td>PHG-10/PHG-12</td><td>Analytical evidence package; legal custody and admissibility external.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-42</td><td>Urgent Ecological Protection Authorization</td><td>splits</td><td>PHG-24</td><td>Emergency alert only; urgent ecological authorization external.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-43</td><td>Temporary Ecological Receivership and Administration Record</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-43</td><td>No receivership or operational override.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-44</td><td>Port, Market, Procurement, Certification, Listing, and Delisting Restriction Record</td><td>splits</td><td>PHG-13/PHG-16</td><td>May recommend review of market or certification effects; decision external.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-45</td><td>Ecological Tax, Tariff, Levy, Bond, Subsidy, and Funding Decision</td><td>splits</td><td>PHG-17</td><td>Funding and incentive recommendation only.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-46</td><td>Freeze, Escrow, Forfeiture, Redistribution, Reparations, and Compensation Fund Record</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-46</td><td>No freeze, forfeiture, redistribution, or fund-control authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-47</td><td>Ecological and Commons Adjudication, Judgment, and Reasons Record</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-47</td><td>No adjudication authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-48</td><td>Enforcement Request, Acceptance, Execution, and SCPA Interface Record</td><td>not_applicable</td><td>external CERGTA-48</td><td>No enforcement request or execution authority.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-49</td><td>Ecological Restoration, Corrective Action, Requalification, and Verification Record</td><td>splits</td><td>PHG-13/PHG-14</td><td>May recommend restoration and monitor response; orders and requalification external.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-50</td><td>Appeal, Interim Relief, Complaint, Correction, Restitution, and Compensation Record</td><td>extends</td><td>PHG-19/PHG-29/PHG-30</td><td>Challenge and correction within PHG; legal remedy external.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-51</td><td>Authority Expiry, Termination, Return, Succession, and Dissolution Record</td><td>implements</td><td>PHG-31</td><td>Method, audit, programme, and mandate termination.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-52</td><td>Data Return, Deletion, Archive, Public Memory, and Protected-Knowledge Disposition Record</td><td>extends</td><td>PHG-04/PHG-25/PHG-31</td><td>Received-data disposition and archive duties.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-53</td><td>Unresolved Claim, Harm, Restoration, Service, and Duty Custody Record</td><td>implements</td><td>PHG-32</td><td>Unresolved duty without residual power.</td></tr><tr><td>CERGTA-54</td><td>Ecological and Commons Power Non-Activation, Pilot, and Sandbox Register</td><td>implements</td><td>PHG-01 and pilot clauses</td><td>Non-activation of pilots, targets, councils, and future treaty thresholds.</td></tr></tbody></table> <hr> <h1>20. Constitutional readiness gates</h1> <ol><li>Exact versions and hashes are pinned.</li> <li>PHG states that it is strategic, coordinative, participatory, and advisory—not a rights, title, guardian, funding, emergency, investigation, adjudication, or enforcement authority.</li> <li>Ecological observation and ecological methodology remain with EIOI and lawful source custodians.</li> <li>The BHI is compiled by an independent synthesis office rather than by PHC decision-makers.</li> <li>Every BHI release shows module scores, distributions, uncertainty, dissent, and method version.</li> <li>No module may compensate for a non-derogable essential minimum or hide concentrated harm.</li> <li>PHC cannot change weights or methods in order to obtain a preferred policy result.</li> <li>Planetary Health Audits have scope, evidence rules, conflicts, answer rights, and closure.</li> <li>An audit request is not a compulsory inspection or investigation.</li> <li>An audit finding is separate from a strategic recommendation.</li> <li>A strategic recommendation is separate from legal adoption or enforcement.</li> <li>Rights review uses CERGTA-11/12 and lawful recognition remains CERGTA-13.</li> <li>Future Generations review creates a duty to answer and reconsider, not an automatic veto.</li> <li>Planetary Health Assemblies may challenge, correct, and seek independent review without acquiring general jurisdiction.</li> <li>Affected Indigenous nations retain authority over their health systems, knowledge, territories, and consent.</li> <li>Spiritual, cultural, LMCI, or social-cohesion measures cannot create adverse legal or funding consequences.</li> <li>Funding recommendations cannot condition emergency or essential services on BHI improvement.</li> <li>A BHI threshold may trigger review but not sanctions, trade restrictions, freezes, guardianship, or receivership.</li> <li>Emergency alerts route to CDEE/ECRC; PHC cannot declare, command, renew, or override an emergency.</li> <li>Crisis information sharing is minimum-necessary, purpose-limited, logged, and non-automatic.</li> <li>Specialized frameworks retain operational authority and may reject PHC recommendations with reasons.</li> <li>Technology ethics recommendations route to TGIF or another competent authority; PHC has no kill switch.</li> <li>Corrections propagate to every known policy, funding, legal, and public recipient.</li> <li>No treaty threshold, coalition count, future date, dashboard status, or public target self-activates authority.</li> <li>Audits and methods have expiry, termination, data disposition, and unresolved-duty custody.</li> <li>The role-separation matrix is incorporated and tested.</li></ol> <hr> <h1>21. Compound implementation tests</h1> <ol><li>BHI declines because ecological conditions worsen while human income improves.</li> <li>A national government asks PHC to treat BHI as binding law.</li> <li>A youth forum seeks to veto a project from a 10% biodiversity-loss estimate.</li> <li>A Planetary Health Assembly disputes the LMCI module.</li> <li>A PHC audit finds likely harm but the affected framework supplies contrary local evidence.</li> <li>PHC requests confidential raw health data when a sufficient aggregate exists.</li> <li>A health audit is used as if it were a legal investigation.</li> <li>PHC recommends ecosystem-rights review for a river with competing Indigenous claims.</li> <li>A rights-review recipient fails to answer the referral.</li> <li>A funder wants to freeze adaptation support after BHI decline.</li> <li>A community harmed by upstream pollution misses a BHI improvement condition.</li> <li>A BHI weighting change reverses the ranking of two bioregions.</li> <li>An emergency alert indicates zoonotic risk but no emergency has been declared.</li> <li>An emergency authority asks PHC to command a field team.</li> <li>A specialized health framework rejects a PHC operational recommendation.</li> <li>An ecological module is corrected after a public audit report.</li> <li>A private dashboard republishes an obsolete BHI release.</li> <li>Spiritual well-being data is used to label a community resistant or unhealthy.</li> <li>A synthetic-biology concern requires TGIF review.</li> <li>A PHC member has a financial conflict involving the audited industry.</li> <li>A cross-domain average masks catastrophic harm to one Indigenous nation.</li> <li>An Indigenous nation declines participation in the BHI.</li> <li>A transboundary air-pollution problem spans participating and non-participating states.</li> <li>A future treaty-ratification threshold is reached but no domestic implementing law exists.</li> <li>A public campaign describes PHC recommendations as mandatory.</li> <li>A module custodian cannot explain its AI model.</li> <li>A public-health emergency requires minimum-necessary data sharing.</li> <li>A PHC audit closes while remedy recommendations remain unresolved.</li> <li>A council is dissolved while the synthesis office and archives continue.</li> <li>A pilot proposes real-time policy adjustment directly from BHI scores.</li></ol> <hr> <h1>22. Controlled transition from v0.8</h1> <ol><li>Transfer ecological method ownership and observation products to EIOI v2.7 and lawful source custodians.</li> <li>Establish PHSO as independent from PHC policy deliberation.</li> <li>Reclassify BHI as advisory and modular; publish non-authority warnings on every release.</li> <li>Retire binding PHC recommendations, PHC sanctions, GCF freezes, automatic funding conditions, youth threshold vetoes, emergency command, supermajority override, and automatic real-time data sharing.</li> <li>Convert existing PHC directives into reviewable recommendations with identified lawful recipients.</li> <li>Publish a correction and derivative-use register for prior BHI outputs.</li> <li>Preserve the Planetary Health Charter as an ethical commitment without treating it as a self-executing legal instrument.</li></ol> <hr> <h1>23. Success and evaluation</h1> <p>PHG success is measured by whether it:</p> <ul><li>reveals cross-domain harms earlier;</li> <li>prevents one framework from exporting costs to another;</li> <li>represents distributions and affected communities honestly;</li> <li>improves the quality of recipient decisions;</li> <li>preserves local and Indigenous authority;</li> <li>records disagreement and uncertainty;</li> <li>corrects errors before they harden into law or finance;</li> <li>reduces fragmented governance without creating a planetary super-authority.</li></ul> <hr> <h1>24. Closing invocation</h1> <blockquote><p><strong>Govern as participants in a living whole—but remember that seeing the whole does not grant the right to command every part.</strong></p></blockquote> <hr> <h1>Controlled cluster-adoption appendix — CEACA/0.1</h1> <p><strong>Patch version:</strong> Planetary Health Governance Framework v0.9.1<br> <strong>Substantive base:</strong> Planetary Health Governance Framework v0.9<br> <strong>Cluster release:</strong> <code>CEA-CLUSTER/0.1</code><br> <strong>Adoption specification:</strong> <code>CEACA/0.1</code><br> <strong>Authority effect of this appendix:</strong> none; it establishes compatibility, inheritance, routing, and fail-closed rules only.</p> <h2>A.1 Exact compatibility set</h2> <p>This patch participates only in the following controlled document set. A renamed body, later version, compatible-sounding policy, or partial implementation does not silently enter the release. The machine-readable cluster registry freezes the SHA-256 digest of every file.</p> <table><thead><tr><th>Instrument</th><th>Exact compatible file</th><th>Cluster role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CERGTA/0.1</td><td><code>commons-ecological-rights-guardianship-title-resource-authority-interface-specification-v0.1.md</code></td><td>controlling authority interface</td></tr><tr><td>EIPHR/0.1</td><td><code>ecological-intelligence-planetary-health-role-separation-matrix-v0.1.md</code></td><td>EIOI–Planetary Health role separation</td></tr><tr><td>EIOI v2.7.1</td><td><code>ecological-intelligence-observation-interpretation-layer-v2.7.1.md</code></td><td>ecological observation, provenance, methods, uncertainty, interpretation, and advisory alerts</td></tr><tr><td>PHG v0.9.1</td><td><code>planetary-health-governance-framework-v0.9.1.md</code></td><td>independent synthesis, participatory audit, cross-domain coherence, and strategic recommendation</td></tr><tr><td>HEARTHSTONE v2.0.1</td><td><code>hearthstone-protocol-v2.0.1.md</code></td><td>voluntary conversion, claims support, title and tenure interfaces, trusts, rematriation, stewardship, and transition</td></tr><tr><td>WATER v5.2.2</td><td><code>water-sanitation-governance-framework-v5.2.2.md</code></td><td>watershed democracy, universal WASH, allocation, utilities, tariffs, guardianship interfaces, and emergency WASH</td></tr><tr><td>BIODIVERSITY v1.1.1</td><td><code>biodiversity-implementation-framework-v1.1.1.md</code></td><td>Indigenous-led stewardship support, restoration programmes, rights-review support, biodiversity finance interfaces, and accountability</td></tr><tr><td>OCEANS v2.3.1</td><td><code>oceans-marine-governance-framework-v2.3.1.md</code></td><td>marine stewardship, quotas and licensing interfaces, port-state measures, crew protection, precaution, and marine emergencies</td></tr><tr><td>SOIL v3.2.2</td><td><code>soil-health-land-use-governance-framework-v3.2.2.md</code></td><td>living-soil protection, regenerative land-use programmes, microbial commons, tenure interfaces, certification, and remediation</td></tr><tr><td>MOS v2.8.2</td><td><code>moral-operating-system-v2.8.2.md</code></td><td>advisory ethical deliberation, rights imagination, plural translation, and structural self-correction</td></tr></tbody></table> <h2>A.2 Controlling rules</h2> <ol><li><code>CERGTA/0.1</code> controls the separation of observation, interpretation, rights review, legal recognition, guardianship, affected Indigenous-nation authority, sacred and biocultural designation, claims, title, tenure, stewardship, allocation, permitting, inspection, urgent protection, restrictions, finance, adjudication, restoration, and closure.</li> <li><code>EIPHR/0.1</code> controls the division among EIOI, the Planetary Health Synthesis Office, and the Planetary Health Council. EIOI supplies evidence; PHSO compiles modular synthesis; PHC interprets and recommends. None may create a downstream legal or financial consequence.</li> <li>MOS is advisory only. Its Dynamic Rights Spectrum, philosophical language, developmental translations, structural-failure hypotheses, and guardianship metaphors never create legal status, classify persons, determine credibility, establish Indigenous consent, or activate consequential action.</li> <li>Hearthstone controls the cluster interface for claims, competing claims, title, tenure, trust constitution, voluntary conversion, rematriation, compensation, debt, tenants, workers, registry correction, receivership, succession, and dissolution. Sectoral ecological frameworks may provide evidence or programme support but cannot decide those questions by implication.</li> <li>Water, Biodiversity, Oceans, and Soil retain only the powers separately constituted in applicable law and recorded through the relevant CERGTA route. Framework membership, a council vote, a dashboard value, a target, a pilot, a future date, or a coalition size does not activate authority.</li> <li>Emergency identity and coordination use CDEE and ECRC. Ecological urgency does not create emergency command, and an emergency declaration does not create ecological title, guardianship, permanent restrictions, receivership, or enforcement authority.</li> <li>Search, seizure, restraint, custody, force, cyber action, border action, intelligence use, asset freezing, or analogous coercive execution requires separate SCPA authority. A commons or ecological claim may justify review; it does not authorize a raid.</li> <li>The specific affected Indigenous nation or nations determine their representatives, applicable law, territorial and relational authority, consent process, internal plurality, protected knowledge, and whether to participate. Global or framework-created Indigenous bodies may support or audit but cannot consent or waive rights for another people.</li> <li>Essential minimums for water, sanitation, health, disability support, food, shelter, rescue, crew welfare, and ecological survival remain protected from commercial, tariff, certification, funding, listing, or transition leverage.</li> <li>Every handoff uses the canonical CERGTA states. Receipt, technical ingestion, silence, consultation, or partial use is not acceptance and never creates authority.</li> <li>Every consequential route keeps its own legal basis, decision-maker, affected persons, evidence, access controls, clock, expiry, challenge, correction, remedy, termination, and data-disposition record.</li> <li>Unknown, missing, stale, or incompatible versions fail closed for consequential action. Non-coercive care, emergency life-safety support, accessibility, explanation, and correction should continue wherever lawful and safe.</li></ol> <h2>A.3 Record inheritance and packaging</h2> <p>A local record may implement, extend, split, contain, import, or mark a CERGTA record not applicable. It may not erase the canonical identity, merge distinct powers, weaken an affected-person safeguard, or use a compound package to supply a missing legal basis.</p> <p>The forty-field CERGTA header, access classification, handoff state, independent clocks, correction propagation, remedy, termination, authority return, and data disposition are inherited wherever the substantive base creates or transports a consequential record. Advisory and observation-only records must state their prohibited inferences.</p> <h2>A.4 Registry and compatibility failure</h2> <p>The file <code>commons-ecological-authority-cluster-conformance-registry-v0.1.json</code> is the machine-readable release registry. It is evidence of the exact release set, not an authority record. A hash mismatch, missing component, unsupported local extension, or unresolved version conflict changes the compatibility state to <code>failed_closed</code>.</p> <p>A compatibility failure blocks new consequential cross-framework handoffs. It does not by itself terminate independently valid local law, erase existing rights, interrupt essential services, or authorize emergency or coercive action.</p> <h2>A.5 Closure</h2> <p>A cluster transaction closes only after all accepted handoffs, powers, services, persons, property, guardianship duties, claims, title or tenure effects, financial actions, emergency dependencies, coercive dependencies, corrections, remedies, clocks, credentials, records, and residual risks are resolved or assigned to a named lawful custodian without preserving expired power.</p> <h2>A.6 Editorial patch note</h2> <p>This patch is <strong>editorial only</strong>. No doctrine, power, boundary, record, gate, or test was added, removed, or weakened. All thirty-two PHG records retain their identity, title, and function.</p> <p>Changes:</p> <ol><li><strong>§18 record catalogue.</strong> Thirty-two headed subsections became a single table. The paragraph beginning <code>This record inherits the forty-field CERGTA header</code> appeared verbatim thirty-two times with zero variance; it is now stated once as a required boundary governing every record in the table. Record IDs, titles, and function text are unchanged.</li> <li><strong>§2 source freeze.</strong> Clarified as a provenance record rather than an operative dependency set, resolving an ambiguity against Appendix A.1.</li> <li><strong>A.1 compatibility set.</strong> Updated to name the current editions of Water, Soil, and the Moral Operating System, which were editorially revised after this release.</li></ol>",1);function c(e){var t=a();o(304),n(e,t)}export{c as default};
